  "slug": "centromere-assemblies-1476",
  "summary": "T2T assemblies make centromeres assessable, and new alignment-free metrics target centromere scoring and length inference from short reads.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational characterization of human centromeres in T2T data, where structure is assessed through sequence distributions and k-mer signatures rather than read or assembly alignment. A mathematical framework scores T2T genome assembly accuracy by comparing centromeric inter-motif distances via KL divergence, without alignment. HORoSCOPE applies k-mer signatures to infer centromere architecture and length from short reads, trained on 11,836 T2T centromeres. What is new is the shift toward distribution-level, alignment-independent descriptors for both evaluating and recovering centromere structure. Two remaining preprints are not centromere-specific: Hyper3D-lite audits count-preserving representations for long-read multi-contact genome data, and topology-aware thermodynamic scores address DNA probe specificity under fixed stringency.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-06-14"
}
